      "explanation": "Correct: (D) 5. pH = −log[H⁺]. Here [H⁺] = 1 × 10⁻⁵ M, so log(1 × 10⁻⁵) = −5 and pH = −(−5) = 5. Shortcut: when [H⁺] is written as 1 × 10⁻ⁿ M, the pH is n. A pH of 5 is acidic (below 7), and its [H⁺] is 100 times greater than that of neutral water at 1 × 10⁻⁷ M.\n\n(A) is wrong because 9 is 14 − 5, which is the pOH of this solution, not its pH; pOH = 14 − pH applies only after pH has been found.\n(B) is wrong because 1 × 10⁻⁵ is the H⁺ concentration given in the stem; pH is the negative logarithm of that number, not the concentration itself.\n(C) is wrong because −5 is log[H⁺] without the minus sign in the pH formula; applying the minus sign gives −(−5) = +5."
