      "explanation": "Correct: (A) Ice would keep sinking as it formed, letting the lake freeze solid from the bottom up. In reality, water's hydrogen bonds lock into a spaced-out lattice when it freezes, so water expands about 9% on freezing, making ice roughly 8% less dense than liquid water, and it floats. Floating ice forms an insulating layer on the surface, and liquid water at about 4 °C (its densest point) stays below, so aquatic organisms survive winter. If ice were denser, each new layer formed at the cold surface would sink to the bottom, exposing fresh water at the surface to freeze and sink in turn. Over a cold winter the lake could fill with ice from the bottom up, killing most of its organisms.\n\n(B) is wrong because floating ice is exactly what insulates the water below; if ice sank, that protection would vanish and fish would be directly threatened.\n(C) is wrong because sinking ice would make freezing more complete, not prevent it; water still freezes at 0 °C no matter where the ice ends up.\n(D) is wrong because ice density affects the lake's physical layering and insulation, not its hydrogen ion concentration, so it would not make the lake permanently acidic."
