      "correct": 0,
      "explanation": "Correct: (A) The G1 checkpoint. The G1 checkpoint (also called the restriction point) is where the cell decides whether to start a new cycle. It checks that the cell is big enough, that nutrients are available, that growth signals are present, and that the DNA is undamaged. If the answers are yes, the cell is generally committed to finish the whole cycle. If not, it can stop and enter G0.\n\n(B) is wrong because the G2 checkpoint comes later and checks that DNA replication is complete and accurate.\n(C) is wrong because the M (spindle) checkpoint comes even later and checks that chromosomes are attached to the spindle before anaphase.\n(D) is wrong because the cell cycle has several checkpoints, but G1 is the main commitment point."
