      "explanation": "Correct: (A) Cyclins bind and activate CDKs, and cyclin-CDK pairs reaching required activity drive progression past checkpoints. A CDK (cyclin-dependent kinase) is an enzyme that adds phosphate groups to target proteins; its levels in the cell stay fairly constant, but on its own it is inactive. Cyclin levels rise and fall at set times during the cell cycle. When a particular cyclin builds up and binds its CDK, the CDK is switched on and phosphorylates the proteins that push the cell past a checkpoint (for example, from G1 into S phase, or from G2 into mitosis). The name says it: the kinase is 'cyclin-dependent.'\n\n(B) is wrong because a cyclin (a regulatory protein) and a CDK (an enzyme) are different molecules.\n(C) is wrong because they work together; a CDK is inactive without its cyclin.\n(D) is wrong because it reverses the roles: cyclins activate CDKs, and cyclins are central to getting past checkpoints."
