      "explanation": "Correct: (A) Reception (ligand binds receptor), transduction (signal relayed), response (outcome). Cell signaling follows three stages in this order. In reception, a signal molecule (ligand) binds a specific receptor, either on the cell surface or inside the cell, and changes the receptor's shape. In transduction, that change starts a relay, such as a phosphorylation cascade of protein kinases or the release of second messengers like cAMP or Ca²⁺, which passes the message along and often amplifies it many-fold. In response, the cell carries out a specific activity, such as changing gene expression, activating an enzyme, opening channels, or triggering apoptosis. Each stage depends on the one before it.\n\n(B) is wrong because transduction cannot begin first; there is no signal to relay until a ligand has bound and activated a receptor.\n(C) is wrong because it runs the stages backward; a response is the final outcome and cannot happen before the signal is received and relayed.\n(D) is wrong because reception, transduction, and response is the general sequence shared by signaling pathways, from hormone signaling to quorum sensing."
