      "explanation": "Correct: (D) AaBb. A homozygous parent has only one kind of allele for each gene, so it can make only one kind of gamete. The AABB parent makes only AB gametes and the aabb parent makes only ab gametes. Every offspring therefore receives AB from one parent and ab from the other, making every F1 individual AaBb: identical and heterozygous for both genes. (This is the first step of a dihybrid cross: crossing two AaBb F1 individuals later produces the classic 9:3:3:1 F2 ratio.)\n\n(B) is wrong because AABB offspring would need the aabb parent to supply dominant alleles, which it does not have.\n(C) is wrong because aabb offspring would need the AABB parent to supply recessive alleles, which it does not have.\n(A) is wrong because variation among offspring appears only when the parents are heterozygous; here both parents are homozygous, so all offspring are the same."
