      "explanation": "Correct: (A) Random fertilization. Random fertilization is the variation that comes from chance at the moment of fertilization: any one of millions of genetically different sperm can fuse with any one of the genetically different eggs. A human parent can already make about 2<sup>23</sup> (more than 8 million) different gametes from independent assortment alone, so two parents can produce roughly 8 million x 8 million = about 70 trillion different zygotes, even before crossing over is counted. The other two sources of variation take place during meiosis inside one parent; random fertilization takes place when gametes from two different parents combine.\n\n(C) is wrong because crossing over creates new allele combinations within chromosomes in prophase I, inside a single meiosis.\n(D) is wrong because independent assortment creates new combinations of whole chromosomes in metaphase I, also inside a single meiosis, not at fertilization.\n(B) is wrong because nondisjunction is a chromosome separation ERROR that produces abnormal chromosome numbers, not a normal source of variation."
