    {
      "id": "6-68",
      "topic": "6.6 Biotechnology",
      "stem": "A circular plasmid is cut with one restriction enzyme and the products are run on an agarose gel next to a size ladder. Two bands appear, one at 2500 bp and one at 1500 bp, and there are no other bands. What can be concluded about the plasmid and the enzyme?",
      "choices": [
        "The plasmid is 1500 bp long and the enzyme cuts it once.",
        "The plasmid is 4000 bp long and the enzyme cuts it at two sites.",
        "The plasmid is 4000 bp long and the enzyme cuts it at one site.",
        "The plasmid is 1000 bp long and the enzyme cuts it at two sites."
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "Correct: (B) The plasmid is 4000 bp long and the enzyme cuts it at two sites. Each band is a group of fragments of one size. A circular molecule cut at n sites gives n fragments, so two bands mean two cuts, and the fragment lengths must add up to the whole plasmid: 2500 bp + 1500 bp = 4000 bp. (A) is wrong because 1500 bp is only one of the two fragments, and the total length is the sum of all fragments. (C) is wrong because a circle cut at a single site opens into one linear piece, which would give only one band at the full length. (D) is wrong because it subtracts the fragment sizes (2500 - 1500) instead of adding them, but the fragments are pieces of the same plasmid and together make up its length.",
      "added": "2026-11-17"
    },
    {
      "id": "6-69",
      "topic": "6.6 Biotechnology",
      "stem": "A crime-scene DNA sample is cut with restriction enzymes and run on a gel, giving bands at 900 bp, 600 bp, and 350 bp. Samples from three suspects are run on the same gel. Suspect 1 has bands at 900, 600, and 200 bp. Suspect 2 has bands at 900, 600, and 350 bp. Suspect 3 has bands at 900, 450, and 350 bp. Which conclusion is best supported?",
      "choices": [
        "Suspect 1 is the source, because two of the three bands match the crime-scene sample.",
        "Suspect 3 is the source, because it shares the 900 bp and 350 bp bands with the sample.",
        "Suspect 2 is consistent with being the source, and Suspects 1 and 3 can be excluded.",
        "No suspect can be excluded, because all three share a band at 900 bp."
      ],
      "correct": 2,
      "explanation": "Correct: (C) Suspect 2 is consistent with being the source, and Suspects 1 and 3 can be excluded. On a gel, bands at the same distance from the wells are fragments of the same size. A match requires every band to line up. Suspect 2 has 900, 600, and 350 bp, exactly the crime-scene pattern. Suspect 1 has a 200 bp band that is absent from the sample, and Suspect 3 has a 450 bp band instead of 600 bp, so each differs and is excluded. (A) is wrong because a partial match is not a match; the extra 200 bp band and missing 350 bp band show a different DNA pattern. (B) is wrong for the same reason: Suspect 3 lacks the 600 bp band, so its pattern is different. (D) is wrong because sharing one band is common between unrelated people and does not make the full patterns identical.",
      "added": "2026-11-17"
    },
    {
      "id": "6-70",
      "topic": "6.6 Biotechnology",
      "stem": "A forensic lab starts a PCR with 50 copies of a target sequence. If every cycle doubles the number of copies, about how many copies of the target are present after 6 cycles?",
      "choices": [
        "300",
        "1600",
        "600",
        "3200"
      ],
      "correct": 3,
      "explanation": "Correct: (D) 3200. Each PCR cycle (denature, anneal, extend) copies every target molecule, so the number doubles: after n cycles there are starting copies x 2^n. Here that is 50 x 2^6 = 50 x 64 = 3200 copies. The growth is exponential, which is why PCR can produce a detectable amount of DNA from a tiny sample. (A) is wrong because 300 comes from 50 x 6, which treats each cycle as adding 50 copies instead of doubling the total. (B) is wrong because 1600 is 50 x 2^5 = 50 x 32, which uses only five doublings instead of six cycles. (C) is wrong because 600 comes from 50 x 12 (multiplying by 2 x 6), which again treats growth as linear rather than as repeated doubling.",
      "added": "2026-11-17"
    },
    {
      "id": "6-71",
      "topic": "6.6 Biotechnology",
      "stem": "A student sets up a PCR tube with template DNA, Taq polymerase, free nucleotides, and buffer, but forgets to add the primers. The thermal cycler runs the normal temperature steps. What is the expected result?",
      "choices": [
        "No target is amplified, because DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of a primer that is paired to the template.",
        "Copies of the target are made, but at random lengths, because the polymerase starts at any position on the template.",
        "Copies of the target are made normally, because the heat at the denaturing step creates free ends for the polymerase to use.",
        "The template will not separate into two strands, because primers are needed to break the hydrogen bonds."
      ],
      "correct": 0,
      "explanation": "Correct: (A) No target is amplified, because DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to the 3' end of a primer paired to the template. Primers have two jobs in PCR: they mark where copying starts and stops (so only the target region is copied), and they supply the 3' end that polymerase requires. Without primers, the template strands separate during the 95 C step, but nothing anneals and the polymerase has nowhere to begin. (B) is wrong because DNA polymerase cannot start a new strand from nothing, so no products of any length are made. (C) is wrong because heat only separates strands; it does not create the paired 3' end that polymerase needs. (D) is wrong because strands separate when heat breaks hydrogen bonds, which does not involve primers.",
      "added": "2026-11-17"
    },
    {
      "id": "6-72",
      "topic": "6.6 Biotechnology",
      "stem": "Ampicillin-sensitive E. coli are mixed with a plasmid carrying an ampicillin-resistance gene and given a brief heat shock. Treated cells plated on a plate with ampicillin form 40 colonies, while untreated cells plated on an ampicillin plate form none. Treated cells on a plate without ampicillin grow as a dense lawn. Which conclusion is best supported?",
      "choices": [
        "Ampicillin caused mutations that made the cells resistant to it.",
        "Only the small fraction of cells that took up the plasmid survived on ampicillin, which shows transformation occurred.",
        "Every treated cell took up the plasmid, because the cells on the plate without ampicillin all grew.",
        "The plasmid is toxic to the bacteria, because untreated cells would otherwise have grown on ampicillin."
      ],
      "correct": 1,
      "explanation": "Correct: (B) Only the small fraction of cells that took up the plasmid survived on ampicillin, which shows transformation occurred. The untreated cells give the control: they do not grow on ampicillin, so the antibiotic kills cells that lack the resistance gene. Treated cells that grew on ampicillin must have acquired the plasmid and its resistance gene. Only 40 colonies grew while a dense lawn grew without ampicillin, so most treated cells did not take up the plasmid. (A) is wrong because the untreated control shows ampicillin does not cause resistance; the gene came from the plasmid. (C) is wrong because growth without ampicillin does not require the plasmid, so it says nothing about uptake, and the few colonies on ampicillin show uptake was rare. (D) is wrong because the untreated cells failed to grow because they were killed by ampicillin, not because of the plasmid.",
      "added": "2026-11-17"
    },
    {
      "id": "6-73",
      "topic": "6.6 Biotechnology",
      "stem": "A PCR primer pair flanks a region of a gene. The normal allele gives a 500 bp product. A disease allele with a 150 bp deletion in this region gives a shorter product. On a gel, Parent 1 shows only a 500 bp band, Parent 2 shows bands at 500 bp and 350 bp, and their child shows bands at 500 bp and 350 bp. Which statement is correct?",
      "choices": [
        "The child is homozygous for the disease allele and Parent 2 is homozygous normal.",
        "The child is homozygous for the normal allele and received the 350 bp band from a mutation.",
        "The child is heterozygous, a carrier, and inherited the 350 bp (disease) allele from Parent 2.",
        "The child is heterozygous and inherited the 350 bp (disease) allele from Parent 1."
      ],
      "correct": 2,
      "explanation": "Correct: (C) The child is heterozygous, a carrier, and inherited the 350 bp (disease) allele from Parent 2. The deleted allele gives 500 - 150 = 350 bp, so the 350 bp band marks the disease allele. A person with both bands has one copy of each allele (heterozygous). Parent 1 shows only the 500 bp band, so Parent 1 has no disease allele to pass on, and the child must have received the 350 bp allele from Parent 2, who is also a carrier. (A) is wrong because homozygous disease would show only the 350 bp band, and Parent 2 has both bands so is not homozygous normal. (B) is wrong because the child has the 350 bp band and so carries the disease allele, which is not a new mutation since Parent 2 has it. (D) is wrong because parent 1 has only the 500 bp band and cannot pass on a 350 bp allele.",
      "added": "2026-11-17"
    }
