      "correct": 3,
      "explanation": "Correct: (D) species with more similar DNA/protein sequences generally share a more recent common ancestor. Mutations accumulate in each lineage after two species split from a common ancestor, so the longer ago they split, the more differences have built up, which is the basis of the molecular clock. For example, human and chimpanzee cytochrome c proteins are almost identical, while human and yeast cytochrome c differ at many positions. All living things also use DNA, the same genetic code, and similar ribosomes, which supports common ancestry for all life.\n\n(A) is wrong because sequence differences track evolutionary relatedness, with close relatives having more similar sequences than distant ones.\n(B) is wrong because different species have different sequences; if all were identical there would be nothing to compare and no differences to measure.\n(C) is wrong because the power of molecular data comes from comparing sequences across species, which is how phylogenetic trees are built."
