      "explanation": "Correct: (C) Rare resistant plants already present survived and reproduced, so resistance alleles became common. The weed population evolved. A few plants that already carried resistance alleles survived the first sprayings and produced seed, while susceptible plants died, so the resistant fraction grew each year. The greenhouse test is the key clue: offspring raised without ever meeting the herbicide were still resistant, so resistance is inherited, not acquired. Herbicide, pesticide, antibiotic, and chemotherapy resistance all show that species continue to evolve under strong selection. (A) is wrong because mutations arise randomly and are not directed by the herbicide; selection only increased resistance alleles that already existed. (B) is wrong because tolerance gained during one plant's life would not explain why offspring raised with no herbicide exposure were also resistant. (D) is wrong because the farmer used fresh herbicide at the same dose, and the offspring of survivors resisted it in a separate greenhouse test.",
